ear Acoustic Properties of Structural Materials. A Review. Springer, Boston, 1990. [25] J. Cantrell. Fundamentals and applications of nonlinear ultrasonic nondestructive evaluation. Ultrasonic Nondestructive Evaluation: Engineering and biological material characterization, 1:363–434, 2004. [26] L. Carvajal, M. Sosa, A. Artigas, N. Luco, and A. Monsalve. Ultrasonic assessment of the influence of cold rolling and recrystallization annealing on the elastic constants in a TWIP steel. Materials, 14(21):65–59, 2021. [27] A.V. Granato and K. Lücke. Theory of mechanical damping due to dislocations. Journal of applied physics, 27(6):583–593, 1956. [28] A.V. Granato and K. Lücke. Application of dislocation theory to internal friction phenomena at high frequencies. Journal of applied physics, 27(7):789–805, 1956. [29] A. Hikata, B. Chick, and C. Elbaum. Dislocation contribution to the second harmonic generation of ultrasonic waves. Journal of Applied Physics, 36(1):229–236, 1965. [30] A. Hikata and C. Elbaum. Generation of ultrasonic second and third harmonics due to dislocations. i. Physical Review, 144(2):469–477, 1966. [31] J. Cantrell and W. Yost. Effect of precipitate coherency strains on acoustic harmonic generation. Journal of applied physics, 81(7):2957–2962, 1997. [32] J. Cantrell and X-G. Zhang. Nonlinear acoustic response from precipitate-matrix misfit in a dislocation network. Journal of Applied Physics, 84(10):5469–5472, 1998. [33] J. Cantrell. Quantitative assessment of fatigue damage accumulation in wavy slip metals from acoustic harmonic generation. Philosophical Magazine, 86(11):1539–1554, 2006. [34] V. Nazarov and A. Sutin. Nonlinear elastic constants of solids with cracks. The Journal of the Acoustical Society of America, 102(6):3349–3354, 1997. [35] D. Hurley, D. Balzar, P. Purtscher, and K. Hollman. Nonlinear ultrasonic parameter in quenched martensitic steels. Journal of Applied Physics, 83(9):4584–4588, 1998. [36] K. Balasubramaniam, Valluri J., and R. P